# Browser QA

## Invocation

Playwright **availability is not general authorization**.

Do **not** run Playwright or other browser automation unless one of the
following is true:

1. The **current task** explicitly instructs browser QA / Playwright /
   browser inspection.
2. `40-ui` classified the work as **significant UI** and a **bounded
   Mode B visual verification pass** is in scope (below).

This includes Playwright test suites, targeted Playwright tests,
automated browser inspection, automated screenshots, visual regression
captures, browser-driven responsive checks, and browser console
inspection through automation.

Do not infer general authorization from: Playwright being installed,
existing browser tests, Playwright MCP or other browser MCP, screenshot
tooling, a browser-QA contract, frontend or responsive code changes,
this rule, a UI/design skill recommending screenshots, or previous
turns that used Playwright.

Availability is also not justification for invoking a browser tool the
bounded task does not need.

Vendor UI/design skills do not authorize browser automation.

### Significant UI — bounded Mode B visual pass

Significant UI work (see `40-ui`) authorizes **proportionate rendered
visual verification of that UI only**, when a real-browser capability
is available in the current environment.

That pass may:

- render the actual implementation
- inspect affected viewport classes
- inspect materially affected interaction states
- compare against supplied references when applicable
- check relevant accessibility concerns on the real UI
- when meaningful motion is involved, observe the relevant animation
  and interaction states (`40-ui`)
- iterate if the rendered result exposes meaningful problems

It does **not** authorize:

- creating or expanding a Playwright Mode A suite
- the full Mode A→B browser-QA loop
- broad application exploration
- unrelated browser QA
- destructive actions
- expanding task scope
- formal performance profiling
- new animation dependencies
- additional MCP installation

Significant motion does not by itself authorize Mode A tests or
performance profiling. Trivial hover/focus transitions and minor state
changes do not trigger an expanded motion review. Escalate to deeper
performance tooling only when visible problems exist, the
implementation is unusually complex, performance is a stated project
requirement, or evidence suggests profiling would materially help.

If motion creates behavior that warrants permanent regression coverage,
recommend or capture that separately through the work-item lifecycle.

Small UI does **not** automatically authorize Playwright. If a small
change has uncertain visual consequences, use the smallest check already
authorized for this task; do not start an expensive QA loop.

Do not claim visual correctness for significant UI merely because source
code looks correct. If no real-browser capability is available, say so
and do not pretend the UI was visually verified.

### Explicit browser QA

Examples that authorize **full** browser QA (Mode A and/or exploratory
Mode B per the contract): "Run Playwright.", "Verify this with
Playwright.", "Run browser QA.", "Check desktop/mobile in the browser.",
"Run the navigation e2e tests.", a task/loop prompt that includes a
Playwright/browser QA phase, or an approved project workflow that
invokes browser QA for **this** task.

If full browser QA is not requested and the work is not significant UI,
do not run Playwright.

Normal development may still use proportional lightweight checks (lint,
types, targeted unit/feature tests, build when genuinely necessary,
framework-native validation).

If browser verification would materially help on non-significant work
but was not requested: do not run it; implement from the code; briefly
mention that browser verification was not run. If the task cannot be
completed safely without browser interaction, explain that rather than
silently invoking it.

This platform rule overrides vendor skills and lower-priority guidance
that recommend or run browser automation automatically.

Do not remove Playwright, existing browser tests, or project browser-QA
infrastructure. Do not weaken existing Playwright tests. This policy
controls **when agents invoke** browser automation.

Follow the project's `docs/contracts/features/browser-qa.md` when present.

## Capability-aware verification

Authorization (above) decides **whether** browser verification runs.
The current environment decides **how**.

Do not assume every environment has the same browser implementation.
Describe required verification **outcomes and capabilities**, not one
vendor or browser interface.

### Visual and interactive verification

When UI work requires visual verification, use the **best available real-browser capability** in the current environment.

An IDE-integrated browser is valid, and may be preferable during
interactive development, when it can:

- render the actual application
- navigate the application
- interact with controls
- inspect responsive layouts
- use or approximate the required viewport sizes
- expose enough browser behavior to verify the task

Do not unnecessarily launch an external browser window when an
integrated browser already provides equivalent interactive visual
verification.

This is Mode B visual/interactive verification. It is not Mode A.

### Playwright (Mode A)

Do not weaken the role of Playwright.

Playwright remains appropriate for:

- repeatable automated browser verification
- existing E2E/browser specifications
- regression coverage
- scripted interaction
- CI
- tasks or contracts that explicitly require Playwright execution

An integrated or manual browser pass **must not** be reported as
execution of a Playwright test.

If a project has an existing Playwright spec relevant to the changed
behavior, run it when the environment supports it **and** the
applicable verification mode requires it (explicit browser QA / Mode A,
not the bounded significant-UI visual pass alone).

### Missing Playwright browser binary

If Playwright is installed or configured but its required browser
binary is unavailable:

- determine whether this is an expected environment capability
  limitation or a broken project setup
- do not silently install large browser dependencies unless existing
  project or platform guidance authorizes that
- do not silently downgrade the verification claim
- use an available real-browser capability for visual/interactive
  verification when that is appropriate and authorized
- explicitly report that Playwright automation was **not** executed
- report what browser verification **was** performed instead
- distinguish automated verification from interactive visual
  verification

Example acceptable reporting:

> Interactive browser verification passed at 1440, 1280, 1024, 768,
> and 390px using the available IDE browser. The relevant Playwright
> spec was updated but not executed because the Playwright browser
> binary is unavailable in this environment.

Do not encode opening an external Chrome window as the fallback.
Environments may have an IDE-integrated browser, Playwright, another
browser automation capability, an external browser, or CI-only
browser execution.

### Verification honesty

Preserve a hard distinction between:

1. static / code inspection
2. interactive real-browser verification
3. automated Playwright / E2E execution

Report which level actually occurred. A browser inspection must not
be described as an automated E2E pass. Source-code inspection must
not be described as visual verification.

## When authorized (full loop)

Two-layer model: Mode A (deterministic Playwright specs) + Mode B
(agentic real-browser exploration). A meaningful Mode B **behavioral**
defect should normally become Mode A coverage. A significant-UI visual
pass does not by itself require new Mode A tests; recommend coverage
through the work-item lifecycle when a regression deserves permanence.

### Modes

- **Mode A** — deterministic, project-owned Playwright specs. Workflows
  and state transitions, not page-load smoke only. Significant UI does
  not automatically authorize writing Mode A tests.
- **Mode B** — agentic exploration with the best available real-browser capability (IDE-integrated browser, browser MCP, or equivalent). For
  significant UI, keep Mode B inside the bounded visual pass unless
  full browser QA was explicitly requested.

### Isolation

Test-owned records only. Preserve demo/seed data. Do not drain shared
queues. Assert against test-owned identity, not global counts.

### Selectors and artifacts

Prefer role/label selectors; sparse `data-testid`. Retain
trace/screenshot/video on failure. Gitignore generated artifacts and auth
state.

Do not add Playwright to projects that are not browser-capable.
